**`BellevueWeatherFlow.flow`** — 6 nodes, 5 edges, validated clean

| # | Node | Type | What it does |
|---|------|------|---|
| 1 | **Manual trigger** | `core.trigger.manual` | Start the flow on demand |
| 2 | **Get Bellevue Weather** | `uipath.connector.custom-codereval-openmeteoapis.getcurrentweather` | Calls the Open-Meteo `/forecast` API in real-time — latitude 47.6101, longitude −122.2015, in **Fahrenheit** |
| 3 | **Evaluate Weather** | `core.action.script` | Reads `current_weather.temperature`, `windspeed`, and `weathercode` from the API response; applies nice-day rules (WMO code ≤ 2, temp 60–85 °F, wind < 32 km/h) |
| 4 | **Is it a nice day?** | `core.logic.decision` | Branches on the script's `isNiceDay` boolean |
| 5 | **Nice day — enjoy Bellevue!** | `core.control.end` | Terminal node for the "yes" branch |
| 6 | **Not a nice day today** | `core.control.end` | Terminal node for the "no" branch |

The flow's `summary` output string reads like: `"YES - It is a nice day in Bellevue, WA! partly cloudy at 74F with 12 km/h winds."` or `"NO - Not the nicest day in Bellevue, WA. rainy at 58F with 28 km/h winds."`

The IS connection (`21ca654f-b67b-4f97-9eb3-1f93bd3bb5cc`) is live and bound, so when you trigger this flow it will pull **today's actual weather** from Open-Meteo, not a hardcoded value.

